1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,800
We came to STFC to make scientific software faster.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,700
Then we asked the agents to make the presentation. Naturally.

3
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:15,600
In cryo-electron microscopy, tiny movements can blur the picture.

4
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:21,700
RELION's motion correction aligns movie frames so the useful signal
adds up.

5
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:27,800
Estimate the motion, shift the frames, then combine them with dose
weighting.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,700
Simple to say. Rather less simple when there are millions of pixels.

7
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:39,700
We took a standalone RELION-derived engine and explored three
acceleration routes:

8
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:45,700
CUDA, JAX and Metal. Different hardware. One very demanding scientific
reference.

9
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:51,800
At this STFC, Google and PA hackathon, Antigravity became our workshop.

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,400
We used it heavily to investigate code, develop changes and iterate.

11
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:59,700
There was still plenty of debugging. Gravity, apparently, still works.

12
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:05,400
We created specialist agents for our Gemini Enterprise workflow.

13
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:10,000
An architect proposes. A critic challenges. Review and testing agents
check.

14
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:14,700
Basically, a very productive meeting where everyone has actually read
the code.

15
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:20,800
On Google Cloud, Gemini-powered workers triage issues, propose fixes
and review pull requests.

16
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:27,700
Small scopes, explicit checks, and humans making the final call. No
automatic victory lap.

17
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:33,800
We tackled thread-dependent randomness and built reproducible
comparisons.

18
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:39,700
The fixed CPU matched across one and four threads on all twenty-four
tutorial movies.

19
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:45,800
CUDA passed the small synthetic global-alignment tests. Real movies
revealed image differences.

20
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:52,700
So we kept the numerical gates, recorded the failures and traced the
pipeline. Science, not vibes.

21
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:58,400
Then came the classic twist: faster GPU kernels, but no whole-movie
speedup yet.

22
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:03,700
The CPU was still doing the expensive reconstruction. We had
accelerated the wrong queue.

23
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:10,800
The newest CUDA pull request moves that reconstruction work onto the
GPU, with streaming buffers.

24
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:16,700
Its early report is promising. Stage timings are not whole-pipeline
speedups. The asterisk stays.

25
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:22,800
Metal now has a global-alignment implementation proposed for Apple
silicon. JAX is an exploration track.

26
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,700
Different stages of progress, shared reference tests. We are building
evidence, not just backends.

27
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:34,400
Our biggest lesson: agents help most when the task and the test are
both clear.

28
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:38,600
Antigravity helped us iterate. Independent checks challenged our
assumptions.

29
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:42,700
The humans supplied the scientific judgment. And the snacks.

30
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:49,000
We leave with GPU prototypes, reusable agents, tougher tests and a much
clearer next step.

31
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:54,600
Then we gave the agents one final issue: make this look good in three
minutes.

32
00:02:55,100 --> 00:02:59,600
Motion, corrected. Hype, tested.
